    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Intonation</span> is the rise and fall of the voice in speech. In English, intonation is not decorative — it changes meaning, signals sentence type, and shows attitude. WAEC's spoken English section tests your ability to identify and apply the correct intonation pattern.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Falling Tone</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">falling tone</span> is used mainly in:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Statements: \"I am going to the market. ↘\"</li>\n  <li>Commands: \"Close the door. ↘\"</li>\n  <li>Wh-questions (who, what, where, when, why, how): \"Where are you going? ↘\"</li>\n  <li>The last item in a list: \"I bought rice, beans and oil. ↘\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The pitch of the voice falls noticeably on the last stressed syllable, giving a sense of completeness or certainty.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Rising Tone</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">rising tone</span> is used mainly in:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Yes/No questions: \"Are you coming? ↗\"</li>\n  <li>Polite requests: \"Could you help me? ↗\"</li>\n  <li>Non-final items in a list: \"I bought rice ↗, beans ↗, and oil ↘.\"</li>\n  <li>Expressing doubt, surprise, or incompleteness</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The pitch of the voice rises on the last stressed syllable, showing that more is expected or that a yes/no answer is needed.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Sentence Type</th><th>Tone</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Statement</td><td>Falling</td><td>She is a teacher. ↘</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Command</td><td>Falling</td><td>Sit down. ↘</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wh-question</td><td>Falling</td><td>What is your name? ↘</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Yes/No question</td><td>Rising</td><td>Is he your brother? ↗</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Polite request</td><td>Rising</td><td>May I come in? ↗</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC often gives a sentence and asks which tone is used, or the reverse (asks you to identify which sentence carries a rising tone). Memorise: Wh-questions fall; Yes/No questions rise.\n</div>\n",
