    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Nouns</span> name people, places, things, and ideas. Mastering noun types and plural formation is essential for accurate writing and is a recurring source of WAEC objective questions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Nouns</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Common noun</td><td>General name for a person, place, or thing</td><td>city, book, teacher</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Proper noun</td><td>Specific name, always capitalised</td><td>Lagos, Chinedu, WAEC</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Collective noun</td><td>Names a group of people or things</td><td>flock, jury, committee</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Abstract noun</td><td>Names an idea, quality, or state that cannot be touched</td><td>honesty, freedom, joy</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Plural Formation – Regular</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Most nouns form plurals by adding <strong>-s</strong> or <strong>-es</strong>: book → books, box → boxes, church → churches. Nouns ending in a consonant + y change y to i and add -es: city → cities. Nouns ending in -f or -fe often change to -ves: leaf → leaves, knife → knives.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Plural Formation – Irregular</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Singular</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>man</td><td>men</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>child</td><td>children</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>mouse</td><td>mice</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>sheep</td><td>sheep (unchanged)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>criterion</td><td>criteria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>datum</td><td>data</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Noun Phrases</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">noun phrase</span> is a group of words built around a noun (its \"head\") that functions together as a subject, object, or complement. Example: \"The tall, quiet boy in the corner\" is a noun phrase whose head is \"boy\".</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Collective nouns can take singular or plural verbs depending on whether the group acts as one unit (\"The team is winning\") or as individuals (\"The team are arguing among themselves\"). WAEC often tests this subtlety.\n</div>\n",
