    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Expository writing</span> explains, informs, or clarifies a topic using facts, examples, and logical reasoning — unlike narrative or argumentative essays, it does not primarily tell a story or persuade. WAEC essay questions on current issues (examinations, elections, drug abuse, corruption) are usually expository.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Purpose of Expository Writing</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An expository essay aims to <strong>explain a topic clearly and objectively</strong>. It answers questions like \"What is it?\", \"How does it work?\", or \"What are its causes and effects?\" — for example, \"Examination Malpractice: Causes and Solutions.\"</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Structure of an Expository Essay</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Introduction</td><td>States the topic and gives background; ends with a clear thesis statement</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Body paragraphs</td><td>Each paragraph explains one point with facts, examples, or statistics</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Conclusion</td><td>Summarises the main points and may suggest a way forward</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Key Features</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Objective, factual tone — the writer's personal opinion is kept in the background</li>\n  <li>Clear topic sentences that begin each paragraph</li>\n  <li>Logical order (chronological, cause-and-effect, or order of importance)</li>\n  <li>Length control: WAEC essays are typically capped (e.g., not more than 400 words) — writing far beyond or below the limit loses marks</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Common Topics</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Typical expository topics include external examinations, elections, plane crashes, excursions, drug abuse, and corruption. For each, candidates are expected to explain causes, effects, and possible solutions using organised paragraphs.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not turn an expository essay into a story. Avoid \"I went to school and saw...\" — instead explain the topic with facts: \"Examination malpractice is the act of...\"\n</div>\n",
