    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Summarizing</span> a talk or lecture means reducing a spoken or written piece to its essential points, in your own words, without losing the original meaning. This differs from summarizing a written passage because you must first listen carefully and capture ideas as they are spoken.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Steps for Summarizing a Talk or Lecture</h3>\n<ol class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Listen attentively to the whole talk before writing anything, if possible</li>\n  <li>Identify the main points, ignoring illustrative stories, repetitions, and filler words</li>\n  <li>Take brief notes of key ideas as the speaker talks</li>\n  <li>Organise the points logically, usually in the order they were presented</li>\n  <li>Write the summary in your own words, in continuous prose (not bullet points, unless instructed)</li>\n  <li>Stay within the given word or sentence limit</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. What to Exclude from a Summary</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Exclude</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Examples and illustrations</td><td>They support the main point but are not the main point itself</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Repetitions</td><td>Speakers often repeat ideas for emphasis; state each idea once</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Introductory remarks/greetings</td><td>Not part of the substantive content</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Your own opinion</td><td>A summary reports the speaker's ideas, not your views</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Sample Topics for Practice</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Talks/lectures on HIV/AIDS awareness, population growth, corruption, and values orientation are common WAEC summary sources. Practice extracting key points such as causes, effects, and recommendations from such talks.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A summary must be written in continuous, grammatically correct sentences using your own words — merely rearranging the speaker's exact words is not true summarizing.\n</div>\n",
