    "topic": "Spoken English – Consonant Clusters",
    "topicCode": "SS3-ENG-07",
    "module": "Spoken English",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Consonant clusters</span> are sequences of two or more consonant sounds occurring together without a vowel between them, either at the beginning or end of a word or syllable. English (unlike many Nigerian languages) allows quite complex final clusters, which learners must practise carefully.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Final Clusters of Two Consonants</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Examples: <strong>banks</strong> /bæŋks/, <strong>sacks</strong> /sæks/, <strong>tasks</strong> /tɑːsks/, <strong>exist</strong> /ɪɡˈzɪst/, <strong>thrusts</strong> /θrʌsts/. Notice how each final consonant must be pronounced distinctly, even though they blend quickly in fluent speech.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Final Clusters of Three Consonants</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Examples: <strong>scripts</strong> /skrɪpts/, <strong>crafts</strong> /krɑːfts/, <strong>twelfth</strong> /twelfθ/. These require careful articulation of each consonant sound in sequence without inserting extra vowel sounds (a common error among learners whose first language avoids such clusters).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Final Clusters of Four Consonants</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Examples: <strong>sixths</strong> /sɪksθs/, <strong>twelfths</strong> /twelfθs/, <strong>prompts</strong> /prɒmpts/, <strong>glimpsed</strong> /ɡlɪmpst/. These are among the most difficult sequences in English pronunciation and require deliberate practice.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Number of Final Consonants</th><th>Example Word</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Two</td><td>banks, tasks, exist</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Three</td><td>scripts, crafts, twelfth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Four</td><td>sixths, twelfths, prompts, glimpsed</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A common pronunciation error is inserting an extra vowel sound between consonants (e.g., saying \"tasks\" as \"task-us\"). Practise saying consonant clusters slowly, then speeding up while keeping every consonant sound intact.\n</div>\n",
