    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Technical vocabulary</span> related to motor vehicles appears often in comprehension passages, instructions, and everyday communication. Knowing the correct term for each vehicle part helps you read and write accurately about transportation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">Key Motor Vehicle Vocabulary</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Chassis</td><td>The frame or base structure that supports the vehicle's body and mechanical parts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bonnet</td><td>The hinged cover over the engine at the front of the vehicle (called \"hood\" in American English)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Trafficators</td><td>Older term for direction/turn indicators that signal a vehicle's intended turn</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Carburetor</td><td>A device that mixes air and fuel for combustion in older petrol engines</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Radiator</td><td>A component that cools the engine by dissipating heat from the coolant</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fuel gauge</td><td>An instrument on the dashboard that shows how much fuel remains in the tank</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Alternator</td><td>A device that generates electricity to charge the battery while the engine runs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wiper</td><td>A blade that moves back and forth to clear rain or dirt from the windscreen</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rear lights</td><td>Lights at the back of the vehicle, used for visibility and braking signals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Side lights</td><td>Small lights on the sides of the vehicle for visibility, especially in low light</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Windscreen</td><td>The front glass panel of a vehicle (called \"windshield\" in American English)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> British and American English often use different terms for the same vehicle part (bonnet/hood, windscreen/windshield). WAEC follows British English, so use the British terms.\n</div>\n",
