    "topic": "Grammatical Structures – Pronouns",
    "topicCode": "SS3-ENG-09",
    "module": "Grammar",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Pronouns</span> are words that stand in place of nouns, avoiding repetition and making sentences flow smoothly. Correct pronoun use — especially case (I/me, who/whom) and agreement — is a frequent source of WAEC grammar questions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Pronouns</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Personal</td><td>I, you, he, she, it, we, they / me, him, her, us, them</td><td>Refers to specific people or things</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Possessive</td><td>mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs</td><td>Shows ownership</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reflexive</td><td>myself, yourself, himself, herself, ourselves, themselves</td><td>Refers back to the subject of the sentence</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Relative</td><td>who, whom, whose, which, that</td><td>Introduces a relative clause, linking it to a noun</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Demonstrative</td><td>this, that, these, those</td><td>Points to specific things</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Indefinite</td><td>someone, anybody, everything, nobody, each, few</td><td>Refers to unspecified people or things</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Subject vs. Object Pronouns</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Subject pronouns (I, you, he, she, it, we, they) perform the action; object pronouns (me, you, him, her, it, us, them) receive the action. Error: \"Me and him went to school\" should be \"He and I went to school.\"</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Who vs. Whom</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Who</strong> is a subject pronoun (\"Who called you?\"); <strong>whom</strong> is an object pronoun (\"To whom did you give the letter?\"). A quick test: if you can replace the word with \"he/she\", use who; if you can replace it with \"him/her\", use whom.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Reflexive Pronoun Errors</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Reflexive pronouns should only be used when the subject and object refer to the same person: \"She hurt herself.\" It is incorrect to say \"John and myself went to the market\" — it should be \"John and I went to the market.\"</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> When a sentence has a compound subject or object (e.g., \"John and ___\"), remove the other person mentally to test the correct pronoun: \"___ went to the market\" clearly needs \"I\", not \"me\" or \"myself\".\n</div>\n",
