    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">The schwa</span> /ə/ is the most common vowel sound in English — a short, relaxed, unstressed vowel sound heard in unstressed syllables. Mastering it is essential for natural-sounding English pronunciation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. What is the Schwa?</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The schwa /ə/ is a weak, central vowel sound, pronounced with minimal muscular effort — like the short \"uh\" sound. It occurs only in unstressed syllables and can be spelled with almost any vowel letter (a, e, i, o, u).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Schwa as the Final Unstressed Syllable</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Many English words ending in <strong>-er, -or, -ar,</strong> or similar unstressed endings are pronounced with a final schwa sound, not a full vowel:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Final Sound</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Teacher</td><td>/ˈtiːtʃə/ — ends in schwa</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mother</td><td>/ˈmʌðə/ — ends in schwa</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Daughter</td><td>/ˈdɔːtə/ — ends in schwa</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sister</td><td>/ˈsɪstə/ — ends in schwa</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Enter</td><td>/ˈentə/ — ends in schwa</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Why the Schwa Matters</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Because it is unstressed and weak, the schwa should never be pronounced as a full, strong vowel. A common learner error is over-pronouncing the final syllable of words like \"teacher\" as a strong \"-er\" sound rather than the relaxed schwa. This affects naturalness and can be tested directly in WAEC spoken English.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Listen for the \"uh\" sound at the end of words like teacher, mother, daughter, sister, and enter — WAEC often asks candidates to identify which word among a group contains the schwa sound in a specific position.\n</div>\n",
