    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Government and administration vocabulary</span> equips you to read and write confidently about civic affairs, an area frequently tested in comprehension passages and essays on national issues.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">Key Government and Administration Vocabulary</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Democracy</td><td>A system of government where power is held by the people, usually through elected representatives</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>National policy</td><td>An official plan or course of action adopted by a government for the whole country</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Revenue</td><td>Income received by a government, usually from taxes and other sources</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>White paper</td><td>An official government report or policy document setting out proposals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Gazette</td><td>An official government publication listing notices, appointments, and laws</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bureaucracy</td><td>A system of government administration run by officials following fixed rules and procedures</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cadre</td><td>A group of trained officials or staff within an organisation or civil service</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Circular</td><td>An official letter or notice sent to many people or departments at once</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Memorandum</td><td>A short official note or record, often used to communicate within an organisation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Agenda</td><td>A list of items to be discussed at a meeting</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Minutes</td><td>The official written record of what was discussed and decided at a meeting</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Permanent secretary</td><td>A senior civil servant who heads the administrative staff of a government ministry</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Distinguish related terms carefully: an \"agenda\" is the plan for a meeting (before it happens), while \"minutes\" is the record of what happened (after the meeting).\n</div>\n",
