    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic combines four skill areas that WAEC frequently tests together: reading and appreciating <span class=\"learn-keyword\">poetry</span> aloud, following spoken <span class=\"learn-keyword\">arguments</span>, writing for different audiences, and using <span class=\"learn-keyword\">adjuncts</span> correctly in sentences.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Spoken English: Reading and Appreciating Poetry</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Reading poetry aloud requires attention to rhythm, stress, pauses (caesura), and tone, since these features carry as much meaning as the words themselves. Practise reading a poem's lines with appropriate pauses at punctuation marks, not necessarily at the end of every line.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Listening: Following Arguments Efficiently</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">When listening to a debate or argument, identify: the speaker's claim (main position), the reasons given to support it, and any evidence or examples used. Distinguishing a claim from its supporting reason helps you follow logical arguments accurately.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Writing for Different Audiences</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The same message must be written differently depending on the audience:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Audience</th><th>Appropriate Style</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Teachers/headmasters</td><td>Formal, respectful, precise language</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Newspapers/magazines</td><td>Engaging, clear, suitable for a general public audience</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Friends/peers</td><td>Informal, conversational tone permitted</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Grammar: Adjuncts – Forms and Functions</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An <span class=\"learn-keyword\">adjunct</span> is an optional element in a sentence that adds extra information without which the sentence is still grammatically complete. Adjuncts can show:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Time:</strong> \"She left <em>yesterday</em>.\"</li>\n  <li><strong>Place:</strong> \"He studies <em>in the library</em>.\"</li>\n  <li><strong>Manner:</strong> \"She sang <em>beautifully</em>.\"</li>\n  <li><strong>Comparison:</strong> \"He runs <em>faster than his brother</em>.\"</li>\n  <li><strong>Result:</strong> \"He worked hard, <em>so he succeeded</em>.\"</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Reading: Paraphrasing Dramatic Works</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Paraphrasing a scene from a play means restating its events and dialogue's meaning in your own plain words, without losing the essential plot or character intentions.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> An adjunct can usually be removed from a sentence without making it ungrammatical — this is a quick test to identify one.\n</div>\n",
