    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic brings together <span class=\"learn-keyword\">paraphrasing</span> skills across listening and reading, vocabulary drawn from Latin and Greek origins, essay revision, and grammar focused on <span class=\"learn-keyword\">adjectival clauses</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Listening: Paraphrasing Poems Listened To</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">After listening to a poem read aloud, you should be able to restate its central meaning and mood in plain prose, without necessarily preserving rhyme or poetic devices.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Reading: Summarizing by Outlining Main Points</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Outlining involves identifying the main heading (overall topic) and sub-points (supporting details) of a passage in a structured, hierarchical list — a useful pre-writing step before composing a full summary.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Vocabulary: Words from Latin and Greek Origins</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Origin</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Democracy</td><td>Greek (demos + kratos)</td><td>Rule by the people</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Biology</td><td>Greek (bios + logos)</td><td>Study of life</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Circular</td><td>Latin (circulus)</td><td>Round; relating to a circle/notice sent widely</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Curriculum</td><td>Latin</td><td>A course of study</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Alumnus</td><td>Latin</td><td>A former student of a school</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Recognising Latin and Greek roots (like \"bio-\" meaning life, \"-ology\" meaning study of) helps you deduce the meanings of unfamiliar words.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Writing: Revising Continuous Writing (Expository Composition)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Revision means reviewing and improving a draft essay: checking that each paragraph has one clear point, correcting grammar errors, and improving word choice — not simply re-writing the same content unchanged.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Grammar: Revising Adjectival Clauses</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An <span class=\"learn-keyword\">adjectival clause</span> (also called a relative clause) describes a noun, usually introduced by who, whom, whose, which, or that. Example: \"The man <em>who called earlier</em> is my uncle.\" Here \"who called earlier\" describes \"the man\".</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> An adjectival clause always modifies a noun (like an adjective would), while an adverbial clause modifies a verb, adjective, or adverb. Check what the clause is describing to tell them apart.\n</div>\n",
