    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic combines listening to <span class=\"learn-keyword\">poetry appreciation</span>, vocabulary connected to <span class=\"learn-keyword\">law and order</span>, writing for varied audiences, and revising <span class=\"learn-keyword\">complex sentences</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Listening: Paraphrasing Poetry</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">As practised earlier, this continues developing the skill of restating a poem's meaning after listening, focusing this time on identifying the poem's tone (the poet's attitude to the subject).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Vocabulary: Words Associated with Law and Order</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Police</td><td>The official body responsible for enforcing laws and maintaining public order</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Plaintiff</td><td>The person who brings a case against another in a court of law</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tribunal</td><td>A special court or panel set up to judge particular types of disputes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Civil rights</td><td>The rights of citizens to freedom and equal treatment under the law</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Justice</td><td>Fair treatment through the law; the quality of being fair and reasonable</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Societal norms</td><td>Behaviours generally accepted and expected within a society</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Writing for Different Audiences (Newspapers, Magazines)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">When writing a letter or article for a newspaper or magazine, use clear, engaging language suited to a broad readership, and consider what background information general readers might need that a specialist reader would not.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Grammar: Revising Complex Sentences</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">complex sentence</span> contains one independent clause and at least one dependent (subordinate) clause, joined by a subordinating conjunction (because, although, since, when, if, while). Example: \"Although it rained, the match continued.\" Here \"Although it rained\" is the dependent clause, and \"the match continued\" is the independent clause.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A dependent clause cannot stand alone as a complete sentence, even though it has a subject and verb — it depends on the independent clause to complete its meaning.\n</div>\n",
