    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic covers <span class=\"learn-keyword\">report writing</span> — a common WAEC essay format — alongside comprehension practice on topical issues and vocabulary focused on <span class=\"learn-keyword\">idioms</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Report Writing: Official and Private Reports</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <strong>report</strong> presents factual information about an event or situation in an organised, objective way, usually for a specific purpose or audience.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example Occasion</th><th>Key Feature</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Official report</td><td>An accident at school, a meeting's proceedings</td><td>Formal, factual, addressed to an authority (e.g., principal, ministry)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Private report</td><td>A club's activity to its members</td><td>Slightly less formal but still factual and organised</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Structure of a Report</h3>\n<ol class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Title/Heading — states the subject of the report</li>\n  <li>Introduction — states what happened, when and where</li>\n  <li>Body — gives full factual details in logical order</li>\n  <li>Conclusion/Recommendation — summarises findings and may suggest action</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Comprehension: Silent Reading of Topical Issues</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Continue practising literal and inferential comprehension questions on social issues, applying the strategies learned earlier in the term.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Vocabulary: Idioms and Idiomatic Expressions</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Idiom</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Silver spoon</td><td>Born into wealth or privilege</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Black sheep</td><td>A person seen as different or disgraceful within a family/group</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bull by the horns</td><td>To confront a difficult situation directly and with determination</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An <span class=\"learn-keyword\">idiom</span> is an expression whose overall meaning cannot be worked out from the literal meaning of its individual words — you must learn the meaning as a whole phrase.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Never translate an idiom literally when explaining its meaning. \"Take the bull by the horns\" is not about actual cattle — it means facing a difficult challenge boldly.\n</div>\n",
