    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic develops the ability to follow and read <span class=\"learn-keyword\">argumentative passages</span> critically, while revising <span class=\"learn-keyword\">adverbial clauses</span> in grammar and applying these skills to writing.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Listening and Reading: Following Argumentative Passages</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An argumentative passage presents a position on an issue and defends it with reasons and evidence, sometimes acknowledging opposing views before refuting them. When reading or listening to such a passage, identify: the writer's/speaker's position, the main supporting arguments, any opposing views mentioned, and the conclusion.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Grammar: Adverbial Clauses</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An <span class=\"learn-keyword\">adverbial clause</span> is a dependent clause that functions like an adverb, modifying the verb in the main clause by showing time, place, reason, condition, purpose, or manner.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Time</td><td>\"She left <em>after the meeting ended</em>.\"</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reason</td><td>\"He stayed home <em>because he was ill</em>.\"</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Condition</td><td>\"You will pass <em>if you study hard</em>.\"</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>\"She saved money <em>so that she could travel</em>.\"</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Place</td><td>\"He stood <em>where the light was brightest</em>.\"</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Concession</td><td>\"He succeeded <em>although he faced many obstacles</em>.\"</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Writing: Applying Argumentative Structure</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">When writing your own argumentative piece, structure it clearly: state your position, give supporting reasons with evidence, briefly acknowledge an opposing view, then explain why your position remains stronger, and conclude firmly.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Adverbial clauses can usually be identified by the subordinating conjunction that introduces them (because, if, although, when, so that, where) — learn these \"signal words\" to spot the clause type quickly.\n</div>\n",
